 Feature screening used the absolute contemporaneous Spearman rank correlation with total precipitation, calculated over the 18,410 raw training-period timestamps from 00:00~UTC on 1 January 2004 to 06:00~UTC on 7 August 2016. These timestamps precede window construction and are distinct from the 18,404 training windows. Spearman correlation measures monotonic association without assuming linear dependence. The seven highest-ranked predictors were retained; validation and test data were excluded from screening. The final inputs were mxtpr, mntpr, lsp, tcc, sp, d2m and v10. This contemporaneous screening rule is distinct from the subsequent lead-time and partial-correlation diagnostics. The screening results are reported in Section~\ref{sec:predictor_selection_results}.
 
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red}
+To examine the mismatch between contemporaneous screening and the six-hour target, a separate training-only diagnostic paired $x(t)$ with $\mathrm{tp}(t+6\,\mathrm{h})$. Contemporaneous correlations use 18,410 training timestamps; lead-time and rank-partial correlations use 18,409 pairs, excluding the last training origin so that its target does not cross the training boundary. These are distinct from the 18,404 windowed training targets. Rank-partial correlations correlate residuals after regressing the ranks of each predictor and the future target on the rank of current precipitation, $\mathrm{tp}(t)$, with an intercept. They control only current precipitation and establish neither causality nor independent model-based predictive contribution. Pointwise 95\% percentile intervals use moving blocks of 28 consecutive six-hourly pairs (seven days), 2,000 replicates and seed 42, with common sampled indices across variables. The original 11-variable pool comprises u10, v10, d2m, t2m, sp, e, mxtpr, mntpr, tcc, ssr and lsp; the expanded pool adds grid-cell wind speed, spatially averaged as ws, and untruncated $\mathrm{cp}=\mathrm{tp}-\mathrm{lsp}$. Within each pool and replicate, candidates are ranked by absolute lead-time correlation. Selection frequency is the fraction entering the top seven; complete-set stability is the fraction whose unordered top-seven set equals the original set. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_associations} and~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_selection_stability} report the separate diagnostics.
+\par}
+
+
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red}
+The saved q21 C6 comparison replaces d2m with cp in the original seven-predictor C0 configuration. Within each architecture, C0 and C6 use the same seven-variable budget, six-snapshot window, hyperparameters, chronological 70/15/15 split, training-only scaling and five seeds (42, 123, 7, 2024 and 99), evaluated on the same 3,939 test targets. Unlike the untruncated cp in the correlation diagnostic, C6 uses cp truncated to non-negative values at each grid cell before spatial averaging; the diagnostic intervals and selection frequencies are not transferred to that processed variable. Paired differences are C6 minus C0 seed-level RMSE, averaged over seeds, not RMSE differences of averaged predictions. Pointwise 95\% seed-t intervals use four degrees of freedom; separate moving-block percentile intervals use 28 consecutive test targets, 5,000 replicates and seed 42, resampling the full series jointly across seeds and configurations before applying subset masks. The latter describe test-sampling uncertainty conditional on the fitted runs, whereas seed-t intervals describe variability across the five matched refits; neither is adjusted for multiple comparisons. Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_c6} provides both.
+\par}
