@@ -1013,6 +1038,11 @@
 
 {\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red,urlcolor=red}
 This experiment is a retrospective ERA5-based hindcast, not a real-time operational forecast. ERA5T is released about five days after valid time, and final ERA5 may replace it after roughly two to three months \citep{ecmwf_era5_documentation}. ERA5 uses 12-hour 4D-Var windows spanning 09--21 and 21--09~UTC; analyses at 00 and 12~UTC can therefore be informed by observations up to nine hours after their valid time. At those forecast origins, precipitation input and target also share an ERA5 short-forecast cycle. Analyses at 06/18~UTC are not an unaffected control: assimilation windows also extend beyond those times. The issue-time check is confounded by time of day and cannot quantify, isolate or exclude these mechanisms. Consequently, valid-time alignment alone does not demonstrate freedom from assimilation-related look-ahead or forecast-cycle dependence, and the reported skill cannot be equated directly with real-time operational forecast skill.
+\par}
+
+
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red}
+The feature-selection evidence is restricted to the tested candidate pools, two retrained architectures and one chronological split. The untruncated-cp screening and cellwise-truncated-cp C6 test are related but not identical variable definitions. The original local predictor set can be retained within these comparisons, but cannot be claimed to be globally optimal; upstream sensitivity also limits conclusions based solely on local marginal rankings.
 \par}
 
 The 6-hour subsampling of the hourly ERA5 data introduces a related constraint. Rapidly developing precipitation systems can evolve on much shorter time scales, particularly during convective events. A six-step input sequence provides 30~h of historical information but cannot resolve sub-hourly or hourly changes in local rainfall structure. Future work could examine whether higher-frequency ERA5 data or complementary radar and satellite observations improve the representation of short-duration events.
