+\AuthorResponse{We agree that the original screening used contemporaneous Spearman correlations and that this alone does not establish six-hour-ahead predictive value. We preserve that historical description and now report a separate training-only lead-time analysis of $x(t)$ against $\mathrm{tp}(t+6\,\mathrm{h})$, using 18,409 pairs from 18,410 training timestamps. Rank-partial correlations control only current precipitation; they are neither causal estimates nor proof of incremental forecast skill. The Methods now specify the common moving-block resampling (28 pairs, 2,000 replicates, seed 42) and the separate definitions of top-seven frequency and complete-set stability.
+
+The precipitation-input correlations fall from approximately 0.90--0.99 contemporaneously to 0.515--0.530 at six hours, close to persistence (0.525). The partial associations are 0.251 (tcc), $-0.188$ (sp), 0.186 (v10) and $-0.024$ (mxtpr). Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_associations} provides all 13 candidates and pointwise intervals. In the original 11-variable pool, the same seven variables are selected and the full set is retained in 99.7\% of 2,000 replicates (unrounded fraction 0.9965). In the 13-variable pool adding ws and untruncated cp, cp replaces d2m, full-set recovery is 0\%, and v10's inclusion frequency is 0.797. These are different candidate-pool results, as shown in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_selection_stability}.
+
+We tested the replacement in saved q21 C6 refits of XGBoost and BiLSTM-Attention, with the same seven-variable budget, hyperparameters, seeds, split, window and 3,939 test targets as matched C0. C6 uses cellwise non-negative truncation of cp before averaging, unlike the untruncated q09 diagnostic; the q09 cp correlations and intervals are not assigned to that processed variable. Overall XGBoost $\Delta$RMSE is $+0.000132$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($+0.054\%$), with both pointwise intervals including zero. BiLSTM-Attention has $+0.001113$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($+0.458\%$), all five seeds worse, seed-t interval $[+0.000592,+0.001633]$ and moving-block interval $[+0.000095,+0.002119]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The latter uses 28 test targets, 5,000 replicates and seed 42, with paired seed-level RMSE differences averaged within each replicate, not averaged predictions. Wet and upper-tail seed-t intervals include zero for BiLSTM-Attention despite positive bootstrap intervals; we do not extend the overall conclusion to all subsets (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_c6}). Neither interval type is multiplicity-adjusted.
+
+We therefore retain the original local seven-variable configuration under the tested conditions, without claiming that the original screening method or variable set is optimal. The separate pre-specified C3 upstream configuration improved overall RMSE by 6.4\% and 7.0\% for the two models, but increased the spatial footprint and input count to 17. It is not a fixed-budget selection test. C3a and C3b were added after viewing C3 and are explicitly exploratory (Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_upstream_xgboost} and~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_upstream_bilstm_attn}). These results indicate useful information outside the local input domain and constrain the scope of retaining the original set.
+
+The additions are in Sections~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset} (diagnostic and matched-refit methods), \ref{manuscript-sec:predictor_selection_results} (results), \ref{manuscript-sec:predictor_complementarity} (interpretation and upstream sensitivity) and \ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}, with the supplementary evidence cited above. No new training or metric estimation was performed for this integration.}
